---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-activecampaign';
const title = `Yotpo Email & SMS to ActiveCampaign: Shopify recovery migration | ${site.name}`;
const desc = 'Evaluate ActiveCampaign after Yotpo Email & SMS: checkout event coverage, sales handoffs, consent records, recovery testing and migration economics.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Can I choose Yotpo Email & SMS for a new recovery flow?', answer: 'Treat this as a migration decision. Yotpo announced Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and its current service notice says the SMS dashboard and background services are decommissioned. Reviews and Loyalty are separate decisions.' },
  { question: 'Does ActiveCampaign recover every Shopify cart?', answer: 'Do not assume every add-to-cart is eligible. Its documentation specifies a Shopify checkout-stage requirement for cart data to sync. Test your current checkout with identified and guest shoppers before estimating reachable volume.' },
  { question: 'Will importing old contacts restart their carts?', answer: 'A contact import is not proof of a new abandonment event or a working checkout link. Use fresh controlled checkouts to validate the trigger; do not replay old records into live reminders.' },
  { question: 'Can I still export from Yotpo?', answer: 'Do not plan around dashboard access. Inventory files already retained, consent evidence and Shopify records. Ask support about unresolved records without promising that recovery or export is available.' },
  { question: 'Should sales staff and automation both follow up?', answer: 'Assign one owner for each buying state. A quote or active conversation should have an explicit routing decision before a consumer reminder is allowed. Test the handoff and the return to automated eligibility.' },
  { question: 'How should incentives be compared?', answer: 'Use net contribution after the actual order discount and variable costs. The worked example on this page is hypothetical, not an observed recovery result or a recommended discount.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Moving Shopify cart recovery from Yotpo Email &amp; SMS to ActiveCampaign</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Evaluate whether checkout recovery can share a customer record with your sales follow-up, without turning every unfinished purchase into both a reminder and a sales task.</p>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The decision has changed</h2>
        <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current service notice</a> confirms that the SMS dashboard and background services are no longer active. This is a replacement evaluation, not a choice between two operating recovery services. Keeping Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty does not establish an active Email &amp; SMS flow.</p>
        <p>ActiveCampaign is worth evaluating when your proposed journey needs a clear boundary between self-service checkout and human assistance. That is a buying criterion, not evidence that your account already implements the handoff. If you only need one checkout reminder, compare the ongoing setup and maintenance burden with a simpler recovery option before adding more branches.</p>
        <h2>What the documented Shopify workflow covers</h2>
        <p>ActiveCampaign's <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001045964-Abandoned-cart-overview">abandoned cart overview</a> requires an ecommerce integration and describes store, product, category and cart-value trigger filters. For Shopify, it specifies that the shopper must reach the checkout information/shipping stage for data to sync. Do not apply an add-to-cart audience estimate to that documented scope.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001046024-How-do-I-create-an-abandoned-cart-automation">automation guide</a> pairs the abandonment trigger with a cart content block in an automated email; that block is not for direct campaigns. Confirm feature entitlement in the intended account and test the rendered message with real test-cart data. A template preview alone cannot establish coverage.</p>
        <h2>Separate the requirements before comparing cost</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Legacy Yotpo material to locate</th><th>ActiveCampaign acceptance decision</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Reachable audience</td><td>Retained trigger definitions and event records, if available</td><td>Which checkout stages actually produce an eligible event?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sales ownership</td><td>Any saved rules for quotes, wholesale or assisted purchases</td><td>Can the intended configuration prevent simultaneous rep and recovery contact?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Permissions</td><td>Channel-specific consent and suppression records already held</td><td>Can unknown or withdrawn permission stay excluded throughout import and sync?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Message continuity</td><td>Saved templates, offer terms and sender records</td><td>Do restored checkout links, variants and sender authentication work in the new flow?</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>A proposed assisted-sale test</h2>
        <p>Use a controlled high-value cart where the shopper asks a rep for a freight quote. Record the cart, contact and quote identifiers separately. Decide whether the rep should own follow-up while the quote is open, and how that ownership becomes available to the automation. An email tag without a maintained source of truth is a fragile substitute for an explicit business state.</p>
        <p>In the proposed test, open the quote before a reminder becomes due. The expected outcome is your chosen exclusion or handoff, not two competing requests to buy. Then close the quote without purchase and verify the deliberate next state: a rep follow-up, a new eligible checkout, or no contact. Do not silently re-enter the original sequence with stale freight or pricing assumptions.</p>
        <p>Run a separate self-service checkout using a different test identity. Confirm the correct store and cart qualify, the intended variant and quantity appear, and the return link reaches the expected checkout. Purchase before the next scheduled touch and inspect the queued action. Repeat with a withdrawn permission and an unavailable variant. Record actual results and timestamps; these are acceptance exercises, not tests performed for this article.</p>
        <h2>Recover the program without assuming old exports exist</h2>
        <p>Start with what you possess: retained files, approved message copy, permission records and Shopify order history. Mark missing consent evidence as unknown rather than treating an email address or past order as permission for every channel. Do not assume a retired dashboard can supply missing subscriber or suppression data.</p>
        <p>Map each field to a destination and an owner. Preserve source and collection context for permission, separate unsubscribed records from missing records, and reconcile duplicates before enabling sends. Keep any existing recovery sender paused or excluded during the handover. Check Shopify's own automations and any SMS provider too; a retired Yotpo service does not prove that all other recovery messages have stopped.</p>
        <p>Rebuild the smallest complete flow first: eligible event, permission check, relevant message, checkout link and purchase exit. Keep browse abandonment, rep tasks and SMS as separate acceptance decisions. Evaluate Sequenzy or another orchestration candidate only if it can demonstrate a needed role against this same event contract; adding a second lifecycle layer does not fix missing checkout data by itself.</p>
        <h2>Timing, channels and economics</h2>
        <p>Inspect both the integration's abandonment wait and any automation delay. Adding a delay without understanding when the event arrives can produce a much later reminder than intended. Set timing from your purchase cycle and support availability. For assisted purchases, the next useful action may be answering the freight question rather than sending a code. For SMS, verify the separate provider, permission evidence, quiet-hour handling and coordination with email before including it in scope.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical incentive example: a $671 order with $402.60 in product cost leaves $268.40 before shipping subsidy, payment fees, returns and messaging cost. A 19% order discount removes $127.49 of revenue. Holding product cost constant leaves $140.91 before those other costs, not $217.40. The discount is applied to revenue, not multiplied into the original margin. This example illustrates the calculation; it is not a recommended offer or a merchant result.</p>
        <p>Request a quote for the intended contact volume and feature set. Include migration work, any sales or SMS tooling and maintenance time in the comparison. Measure eligible checkouts, successful sends, purchases and contribution separately; rep-assisted orders should remain identifiable. If volume supports a randomized holdout, choose its size from a measurement plan rather than copying a universal percentage. Platform-attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental recovery.</p>
        <h2>When to proceed</h2>
        <p>Proceed with ActiveCampaign only when the intended account demonstrates the checkout event, cart email and ownership rules your store needs. If sales ownership cannot be represented reliably, simplify the flow or resolve that gap before activation. Keep a written rollback plan for the new configuration and a named operator for failures; a retired Yotpo service is not a fallback sender.</p>
        <p>Evidence checked November 24, 2026. Supplier documentation supports the narrow workflow claims above. Account access, plan entitlement, delivery, consent handling and live checkout behavior still require validation in your own environment.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Yotpo to ActiveCampaign migration FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
